---

`@ogygia/core` is the code behind `ogygia`: the region compiler, the runtime, the server handle and the plugin API. Apps don't install it themselves. They install [`ogygia`](/docs/start/install), which depends on it and re-exports it subpath for subpath, so `ogygia/server` and `@ogygia/core/server` are the same module.

The other packages (content, flags, postrender, federation, the server router, devtools, testing) take `@ogygia/core` as a peer dependency and build on its plugin API. This section is for people who write or read those packages.

## Install

In a plugin package, list it as a peer dependency, so the app's own copy (brought in by `ogygia`) is the one your code runs against:

```json title=package.json
{
  "name": "my-ogygia-plugin",
  "type": "module",
  "exports": { "./vite": "./src/vite.ts" },
  "peerDependencies": { "@ogygia/core": "^0.11.0" }
}
```

::: warning First-party, not stable
The plugin API is public so that you can read how the packages work, but it is first-party only for now, and its shape can change in any release.
:::

## A first plugin

A plugin is a Vite plugin made with `definePlugin`, and the server half it names runs inside the app's `ogygia.handle()`. Its `name` is the package name, and it must resolve from the app:

```ts title=src/vite.ts
import { definePlugin } from '@ogygia/compiler/vite';

export function poweredBy() {
  return definePlugin({
    name: 'my-ogygia-plugin',
    server: import.meta.resolve('./server.ts')
  });
}
```

```ts title=src/server.ts
import { defineServer } from '@ogygia/core/plugin';

export default defineServer({
  // A returned Response replaces the page's.
  async response(ctx, res) {
    const headers = new Headers(res.headers);
    headers.set('x-powered-by', 'ogygia');
    return new Response(res.body, { status: res.status, headers });
  }
});
```

The app lists it like any other package, beside `ogygia()`:

```ts title=vite.config.ts
plugins: [ogygia(), poweredBy(), sveltekit()]
```

A server half can be run in a unit test without a server, through `startPlugin` from `@ogygia/core/plugin/testing`.

## With ogygia's regions

A plugin can mint regions on the server. `region`, `htmlRegion` and `deferredRegion` from `@ogygia/core/plugin` are the same values an app renders with `<Region>`, which is how [federation](/docs/federation/overview) turns another app's page into a region on yours. Shared hooks (`app.notify.invalidate(tags)`) and request facts (`reads`, `storable`) are how packages cooperate: a flag read lands in `reads`, and [postrender](/docs/postrender/overview) and the hole render cache read it before they keep a render.

